The U.S. state of [[Texas]] first required its residents to register their [[motor vehicle]]s in 1907. Registrants provided their own [[Vehicle registration plate|license plate]]s for display, with serial numbers assigned by their county of residence, until the state began to issue plates in 1917.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://leatherlicenseplates.com/old-texas-license-plates-vintage-texas-license-plates/ |title=Old Texas License Plates |publisher=LeatherLicensePlates.com |date=<!-- Deny Citation Bot--> |access-date=September 3, 2017 |archive-date=September 4, 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170904013733/http://leatherlicenseplates.com/old-texas-license-plates-vintage-texas-license-plates/ |url-status=dead }}</ref>

{{As of|2025}}, plates are manufactured at the [[Wynne Unit]] in [[Huntsville, Texas]], and are issued by the [[Texas Department of Motor Vehicles]] (TxDMV).<ref>{{cite web |title=Plates and Prisoners |url=https://100plates.txdmv.gov/page/Plates_and_Prisoners.html |publisher=Texas Department of Motor Vehicles |access-date=September 24, 2023}}</ref> Front and rear plates are required for most classes of vehicles, while only rear plates are required for motorcycles, trailers and antique vehicles.<ref>{{cite web |title=Application for Antique License Plate (Form VTR-54) |url=https://d3eaozktcyljdh.cloudfront.net/cdn/ff/z1eHVQJa2GfGpn2p1-aAuWyCCUsvM8KKvZ1qfUiPHjI/1599528546/public/2020-09/antique.pdf |publisher=Texas Department of Motor Vehicles |access-date=November 10, 2020 |archive-date=November 10, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201110214433/https://d3eaozktcyljdh.cloudfront.net/cdn/ff/z1eHVQJa2GfGpn2p1-aAuWyCCUsvM8KKvZ1qfUiPHjI/1599528546/public/2020-09/antique.pdf |url-status=dead }}</ref>

==Passenger baseplates==
===1917 to 1974===
In 1956, the United States, Canada, and Mexico came to an agreement with the [[American Association of Motor Vehicle Administrators]], the [[Automobile Manufacturers Association]] and the [[National Safety Council]] that standardized the size for license plates for vehicles (except those for [[motorcycle]]s) at {{convert|6|in|cm}} in height by {{convert|12|in|cm}} in width, with standardized mounting holes.<ref>{{cite magazine |last=Garrish |first=Christopher |date=October 2016 |title=Reconsidering the Standard Plate Size |magazine=Plates |publisher=[[Automobile License Plate Collectors Association]] |volume=62 |number=5}}</ref> The first Texas license plate that complied with these standards was a modification of the 1955 plate, introduced towards the end of that year.
